15. GLOSSARY OF ATTACK TYPES
================================================================================

Double Extortion:
  Victim's files are encrypted AND sensitive data is exfiltrated and threatened 
  with public release if ransom isn't paid.

Triple Extortion:
  Double extortion PLUS additional pressure (DDoS attacks, threatening victims' 
  customers/partners, stock manipulation, etc.).

Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS):
  Business model where developers create ransomware and recruit "affiliates" to 
  deploy it. Profit split between developers and affiliates (typically 70/30 or 80/20).

Data Extortion (Encryption-Free):
  No encryption performed - pure threat of leaking stolen data. 
  (BianLian shifted to this model in 2023)

LOLBins (Living Off the Land Binaries):
  Using legitimate system/admin tools (PsExec, PowerShell, RDP, etc.) to 
  conduct malicious activity - harder to detect than custom malware.

Initial Access Broker (IAPT):
  Specialized threat actor who compromises networks and sells access to 
  ransomware groups. (e.g., QakBot operators → Black Basta)

Kerberoasting:
  Attack against Active Directory Kerberos tickets to crack service account 
  passwords offline.

SIM Swapping:
  Social engineering mobile carrier to transfer victim's phone number to 
  attacker-controlled SIM, enabling MFA bypass.

MFA Fatigue / Push Bombing:
  Sending repeated MFA push notifications until victim accidentally approves.

Drive-by Compromise:
  Malicious content delivered via compromised websites or fake update prompts.

Supply Chain Attack:
  Compromising a trusted third-party tool/vendor to reach ultimate targets.
  (e.g., CL0P exploiting MOVEit, Kaseya VSA attacks)

